    "q-131152": {
      "dom_id": "step-q131152",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "The continuous random variable $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}},\\,1<x<2\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,$ compute $\\text{E}[2X-5{X}^{2}].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that $\\text{E}[aX+bY]=a\\text{E}[X]+b\\text{E}[Y]$ for any random variables $X$ and $Y$ and constants $a$ and $b.$*",
      "worked_solution": "First, we simplify using the hint and the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[2X-5{X}^{2}]=2\\text{E}[X]-5\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe already know $\\text{E}[X].$ So, we compute the second raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2x\\,{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\text{E}[X]=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ and $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=2$ into the simplified expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[2X-5{X}^{2}] & =2\\text{E}[X]-5\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-5\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
