    "q-90454": {
      "dom_id": "step-q90454",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "The continuous random variable $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=4{x}^{3},\\,0<x<1\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{4}{5},$ compute $\\text{E}[9{X}^{2}+X].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that $\\text{E}[aX+bY]=a\\text{E}[X]+b\\text{E}[Y]$ for any random variables $X$ and $Y$ and constants $a$ and $b.$*",
      "worked_solution": "First, we simplify using the hint and the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[9{X}^{2}+X]=9\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]+\\text{E}[X]\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe already know $\\text{E}[X].$ So, we compute the second raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot 4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{2{x}^{6}}{3}\\,|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{4}{5}$ and $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=\\frac{2}{3}$ into the simplified expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[9{X}^{2}+X] & =9\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]+\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & =6+\\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{34}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-90454-a",
